CARDS = [
("frequent", "Frequent", "Why does a synchronous function block your whole server in Node but not in Java?", "Java's thread-per-request server blocks one thread and the others keep serving. Node has one thread running all JavaScript, and the event loop is cooperative — it cannot preempt a running callback, so it regains control only when the function returns. A 400 ms <code>JSON.parse</code> delays every connection in the process. The kernel scheduler still preempts the thread, which protects the rest of the machine but does nothing for your other requests."),
("frequent", "Frequent", "How does a database connection pool relate to threads?", "It is a bounded thread pool with all the same properties. Twenty connections serving two hundred concurrent requests means one hundred and eighty waiting — inside your process, invisibly, unless you instrument acquisition wait time. And one slow query holds a connection while everything behind it queues, which is head-of-line blocking. That is why statement timeouts matter and why separate pools for fast and slow work is a real design."),
("frequent", "Frequent", "Why does Postgres need a connection pool at all?", "It uses one operating system process per connection. Each costs a process control block, several megabytes of private memory, and a scheduler entry, so a few hundred is a genuinely heavy load on the machine — which is why <code>max_connections</code> is 100 to 200, not 10,000. That is also why pgbouncer exists, multiplexing thousands of client connections onto a few dozen real ones."),
("frequent", "Frequent", "What is the difference between process memory (RSS) and heap memory in Node?", "<code>heapUsed</code> is V8's managed heap only. RSS is everything resident: the heap, plus Buffers and ArrayBuffers which live outside it, plus the node binary and shared libraries, plus JIT code space, plus thread stacks, plus allocator fragmentation. That gap is routinely hundreds of megabytes, and the kernel kills on RSS — which is why a heap cap must be set well below the container limit."),
("frequent", "Frequent", "Why does my Node service memory graph go up and never come down?", "Usually not a leak. The allocator does not return freed memory to the kernel — it keeps it in its own free lists for reuse — so RSS is a high-water mark rather than a current reading. A genuine leak is a continuously rising curve under steady load, not a plateau after a spike. If it is genuinely rising with a flat heap, look at Buffers or native addons, not at JavaScript objects."),
("frequent", "Frequent", "What is copy-on-write and where have you seen it?", "Sharing pages read-only between two parties and only copying a page when one writes to it. It is what makes <code>fork()</code> cheap — a 2 GB process forks in a millisecond. Above the kernel, it is Docker image layers copying a file up on first write, Postgres MVCC writing a new row version instead of overwriting, Git's immutable objects making branches free, and React state updates sharing untouched subtrees."),
("frequent", "Frequent", "Why can two containers both listen on port 3000?", "Each has its own network namespace, which includes a private port number space. That is also why containers in the same Kubernetes pod <em>cannot</em> both bind 3000 — they share one network namespace, which is exactly what makes them reachable on localhost."),
("frequent", "Frequent", "What is a namespace and what is a cgroup?", "A namespace changes what a process can <em>see</em> — its own process table, mount tree, network stack, hostname, or user ID mapping. A cgroup limits what it can <em>use</em> — CPU share or quota, memory ceiling, I/O bandwidth, process count. You need both: a namespaced process with no cgroup can still consume the whole machine."),
("frequent", "Frequent", "Why does my container ignore docker stop?", "PID 1 is special: the kernel does not deliver signals with default actions to it, so if your process has not installed a SIGTERM handler the signal is silently discarded, and ten seconds later you get SIGKILL. Or your entrypoint is a shell — <code>CMD npm start</code> becomes <code>sh -c</code> — and the shell does not forward signals to its child. Fix: exec-form entrypoint straight to node, and an explicit SIGTERM handler."),
("frequent", "Frequent", "What does fsync do and why is it slow?", "A <code>write</code> only puts bytes in the kernel's page cache; they survive a process crash but not a power cut. <code>fsync</code> forces them out of the page cache and issues a flush to the device's own write cache, returning only when the device says they are durable. That is milliseconds on a disk and tens to hundreds of microseconds on an SSD — and it is the floor on how fast a database can commit."),
("frequent", "Frequent", "How does a write-ahead log work and why does every database have one?", "Modifying a row means a random write, possibly across several pages, which cannot be made atomic. So you append a description of the change to a sequential log, fsync that once, and report the commit; the data pages get updated lazily afterwards, and a crash is recovered by replaying the log. One random write becomes one sequential append plus one fsync. Postgres WAL, InnoDB redo, SQLite WAL mode, Kafka, etcd's Raft log — all the same shape."),
("frequent", "Frequent", "Why is sequential disk access so much faster than random?", "On a spinning disk a random read is a seek plus half a rotation, around 8 to 12 milliseconds, while sequential transfer runs at 100 to 200 MB/s once positioned — roughly a hundred to one. That ratio is why B-trees keep seeks logarithmic, why write-ahead logs convert random writes into sequential ones, and why the query planner will pick a sequential scan over an index past a certain selectivity. On SSD the ratio collapses, which is why lowering <code>random_page_cost</code> to about 1.1 changes plans."),
("frequent", "Frequent", "What is head-of-line blocking?", "One item at the front of a queue stopping everything behind it, even when the rest could proceed. It is a thread pool where all threads are on slow requests; it is HTTP/1.1 where one response must finish before the next; it is TCP under HTTP/2, where one lost packet stalls every multiplexed stream. HTTP/3 moved to QUIC over UDP specifically to fix that last one."),
("frequent", "Frequent", "Explain the difference between setTimeout, setImmediate, process.nextTick and a promise.", "<code>nextTick</code> and microtasks are not phases at all — they drain completely after every callback and between every phase, nextTick first. <code>setImmediate</code> runs in the check phase, right after poll. <code>setTimeout</code> runs in the timers phase at the start of the next iteration. So after an await, the continuation runs before any timer or I/O. And at the top level, setTimeout(f,0) versus setImmediate is genuinely nondeterministic, because the 1 ms clamp may or may not have elapsed by the time the loop first reaches timers."),
("frequent", "Frequent", "Does JavaScript have race conditions?", "It has no data races, because one callback runs to completion before the next. It absolutely has race conditions, because every <code>await</code> is a yield point — the loop runs other code, including another invocation of the same function, and you resume against changed state. Read a balance, check it, await, write it back: two concurrent calls both pass the check. The fixes are the same as in a threaded program: make it atomic in the database, take a lock, or use a version column."),
("frequent", "Frequent", "What is backpressure and why does it matter?", "It is the signal that a consumer is slower than a producer. In a pipe or a TCP socket it is built in — writing to a full buffer blocks or returns short. In Node, <code>stream.write()</code> returning false is that signal, and the <code>drain</code> event clears it. Ignoring it means Node buffers unwritten data in your heap without limit, which is how streaming a large file to a slow client exhausts memory. <code>pipeline()</code> handles it for you, which is the real reason to use it."),
("frequent", "Frequent", "What does epoll do that select doesn't?", "select rebuilds and re-passes the whole watch set on every call, and the kernel scans all of it — so cost is proportional to how many connections you have rather than how many events happened, which is exactly backwards. epoll keeps the set in the kernel; when data arrives, the socket's wait-queue callback pushes that descriptor onto a ready list, so <code>epoll_wait</code> just hands back the list. A hundred thousand idle connections cost nothing to wait on."),
("frequent", "Frequent", "Why does Node use a thread pool for file I/O but not for sockets?", "Because readiness notification does not work for regular files. epoll answers 'will this block?', and for a file the answer is always no — the real question is how long, which readiness cannot express. epoll returns EPERM for regular files and O_NONBLOCK is ignored on them. So libuv hands file operations to a four-thread pool. io_uring, which is completion-based rather than readiness-based, is the first interface that fixes this properly, and libuv uses it for some file operations since 1.45."),
("frequent", "Frequent", "What is the difference between a hard link and a symbolic link?", "A hard link is another directory entry pointing at the same inode — equally the file, sharing permissions, and the data is freed only when the last one is removed and nobody has it open. A symlink is a tiny file containing a path string, resolved on every use, so it can cross filesystems and can dangle. This is also why deleting a log file a process still has open frees no disk space."),
("frequent", "Frequent", "How do Docker image layers work?", "Each filesystem-changing Dockerfile instruction produces one layer — a directory of differences, content-addressed. A running container stacks them read-only under one writable layer using overlayfs. Reading searches down the stack; writing to a lower-layer file copies the whole file up first; deleting creates a whiteout marker. That is why layers are shared across containers, why deleting a file in a later instruction does not shrink the image or remove a leaked secret, and why instruction ordering is cache management."),
]
